diff --git a/CLAUDE.md b/CLAUDE.md
index b40d209e..b73f95c5 100644
--- a/CLAUDE.md
+++ b/CLAUDE.md
@@ -93,7 +93,7 @@ Key conventions:
 - **Per-sandbox HOME — the carrier launcher never uses a shared /tmp (#393).** The hosted sandbox has NO userns jail (uid+cgroup only), so its `/tmp` is the shared pod `/tmp`. The `python-stack` carrier `launch` used to hardcode `HOME=/tmp`; a user's `pip install --break-system-packages X` can't write the root-owned carrier venv, so pip defaults to `--user` = `$HOME/.local` = `/tmp/.local` — a pod-wide dir every sandbox's carrier python then imports (the cross-sandbox leak). Fix: `_exec_argv` passes `SANDBOX_HOME` to the launcher — unjailed → a per-sandbox `.home` (a `root/` workspace **sibling** in the infra area, so it's never walked/synced and is reaped with the sandbox; `IsolatedProcessSandbox._provision` chowns it to the sandbox uid, 0700); jail → `/tmp` **explicitly** (its `/tmp` is a per-exec ephemeral tmpfs, isolated). The launcher's unset fallback is a PRIVATE `mktemp -d`, NEVER the shared `/tmp` — the default must not be the footgun. `SANDBOX_HOME` survives the `setpriv` uid drop (no `--reset-env`). npm/cargo never leaked (their HOME is already the per-sandbox workspace) and system dirs are uid-blocked, so python was the only leak. `_PYTHON_LAUNCH`/`_LAUNCH` are folded into the `.built` stamp (`_builder_fingerprint`) so a launcher-template edit forces a bundle rebuild (`_source_hash` can't see builder code baked into the bundle — same stale-cache class as #64). Both `local_process.py` + both `isolated_process.py` copies.
 - **Per-item OS-user + cgroup isolation (#345, opt-in).** When item dirs are siblings on one shared vol, `sandbox.isolation.enabled` swaps `LocalProcessSandbox` for `IsolatedProcessSandbox` (`sandbox/isolated_process.py`): each item's `exec` drops (via `setpriv`) to a STABLE uid `uid_base + xxhash(item_id) % uid_range` — same on every pod, so ownership + cgroup are coordination-free — under a per-item cgroup v2 slice. NOT the userns jail (`sandbox.isolate`); a different model (no namespaces). `enabled` None=auto (on iff CAP_SETUID + writable cgroup root), True=fail-loud-if-unsupported, False=off; uv-run forces off. Needs the pod to carry CAP_SETUID/SETGID + a delegated cgroup root (`kubernetes/base/deployment.yaml`). Every privileged op is seamed so the module is 100% unit-covered non-root; real enforcement is a root-gated integration test.
 - **AgentRunner Protocol** is the swap point between scripted tests and live LLM. Tests use `ScriptedAgentRunner`; production uses `LitellmAgentRunner`. The **KB agent reuses the same runner** — `AgentToolContext` serves both flavours (app workspace agent: sandbox/filestore/sync + file/exec tools; KB agent: retriever + collection_ids + `kb_search`, no sandbox), so the workspace-only fields are optional.
-- **Chat turns run through one `ChatTurnEngine`** (`api/turns.py`) shared by app workspace chat + KB chat: per-conversation lock, one in-flight turn per conversation — **both surfaces `enqueue`**, so a new message QUEUES behind the running turn and only an explicit Stop cancels. KB chat used to `stream()` (whose first act is `_cancel_prior_turn`), which threw away an answer the moment a follow-up was asked; its composer hid that by refusing to send at all, so the gesture produced *no reaction whatsoever* — no bubble, no cleared box, no reason. Both halves are gone: the KB send publishes `UserMessage`, enqueues, awaits its own turn to `send_await_timeout` (`shield`, so detaching is not cancelling) and answers **202**, with the live events on `GET /kb/chats/{id}/stream` (a `subscribe_sse` wrapper, gated on `read_chat`) — a POST body held open until the answer finished could not also let the next message queue behind it. `stream()` survives only for callers with no queue semantics of their own. **A queued thread reads Q1, Q2, A1, A2 — deliberately, and do not "fix" it by moving the write.** The question is persisted when it is ACCEPTED (`chat_send.py:795`, long before `enqueue` at :1107), so a thread ordered by creation time puts both questions above both answers. Persisting it when its turn *starts* instead would restore the pairing and break the rule the 202 rests on — the write IS the acceptance, so a non-2xx means nothing was stored — and would lose a queued question outright if the pod died before its turn ran. Re-pairing at render is worse: nothing on a persisted message says which turn produced it, so "the Nth answer belongs to the Nth question" is an invention that tool messages, `role="error"` endings, notices and multi-message answers all break silently. A turn id on the persisted message is the prerequisite for changing this at all. Also the `_drive` pump (CancelledError → `RunCancelled`, other → `RunError`), the SSE `gen()` that reduces events into neutral `TurnMessage`s, and `cancel()`/`forget()`. Each surface only builds its `AgentToolContext` and passes an `on_complete` that maps `TurnMessage` → its model (`Message` / `KbMessage`). `InvestigationRegistry` owns only the **sandbox** lifecycle. Don't reimplement turn/cancel/SSE per surface — extend the engine.
+- **Chat turns run through one `ChatTurnEngine`** (`api/turns.py`) shared by app workspace chat + KB chat: per-conversation lock, one in-flight turn per conversation — **both surfaces `enqueue`**, so a new message never throws away the running turn's work — what it does to that turn is the interjection rule below. KB chat used to `stream()` (whose first act is `_cancel_prior_turn`), which threw away an answer the moment a follow-up was asked; its composer hid that by refusing to send at all, so the gesture produced *no reaction whatsoever* — no bubble, no cleared box, no reason. Both halves are gone: the KB send publishes `UserMessage`, enqueues, awaits its own turn to `send_await_timeout` (`shield`, so detaching is not cancelling) and answers **202**, with the live events on `GET /kb/chats/{id}/stream` (a `subscribe_sse` wrapper, gated on `read_chat`) — a POST body held open until the answer finished could not also let the next message queue behind it. `stream()` survives only for callers with no queue semantics of their own. **A message sent while a turn runs is answered by the first turn that READS it, and the thread records it where it was read (`docs/plan-interjection.md`).** Its author's own message is read by the running turn before its next model call (the runner's `call_model_input_filter` → `Interjected`; the turn continues as a new SDK segment, so `max_turns` and the search allowances start over); another person's message STOPS the running turn (`RunCancelled(by=…)`, persisted as `error_kind="interrupted"` with that person as `author`) and theirs takes over — a turn runs as ONE person, never on a borrowed identity. A message no running turn read waits in the queue; when the worker reaches it, it takes EVERY unseen message waiting (`QueuedMessage`; the last one is the turn, the earlier ones lead it as `ctx.leading_interjections`) and rebuilds the history then (`refresh` → `thread_without`) — every chat turn does, leaving out every message nobody has answered yet (still preparing: `PendingTurn.at`; still queued; owed on another pod: an open claim — app chat only; the KB chat opens no claims, so there it is this pod's record alone) — because a question persisted at acceptance sits ABOVE the answer it waited for. A turn that is stopped (Stop or a takeover) answered nothing it read: those messages go back to the head of the queue, owed. A goal round (`QueuedMessage.driven`) is not a person speaking: it never takes a turn over and is never read or folded into one. The question is still persisted when it is ACCEPTED (`chat_send._send`) — the write IS the acceptance, so a non-2xx means nothing was stored, and a queued question survives the pod; do not move that write. What moves is the POSITION: the turn's output carries a `PLACE_ROLE` marker where it read each message, and the surface's persist moves that already-stored message there (`take_person_message`) — only when something it did not see sits after it (`must_move`; a notice written about its own turn does not count). The position is MEASURED at the moment the turn read it, never re-paired at render — "the Nth answer belongs to the Nth question" is still an invention that tool messages, error endings and notices break. A turn queued with no `QueuedMessage` (a workflow step) is one turn per entry, as before. Also the `_drive` pump (CancelledError → `RunCancelled`, other → `RunError`), the SSE `gen()` that reduces events into neutral `TurnMessage`s, and `cancel()`/`forget()`. Each surface only builds its `AgentToolContext` and passes an `on_complete` that maps `TurnMessage` → its model (`Message` / `KbMessage`). `InvestigationRegistry` owns only the **sandbox** lifecycle. Don't reimplement turn/cancel/SSE per surface — extend the engine.
 - **SSE event schema** (`api/events.py`) is mirrored in `web/src/events.ts`. Keep them in sync when adding event types. The KB chat streams the same events, and the FE renders both chats with the shared `web/src/components/AgentEntryView.tsx` (reasoning / tool cards / metrics).
 - **`kb_search` vs `ask_knowledge_base` — leaf vs consumer-interface; they don't merge (#270).** `kb_search` is the KB agent's own retrieval *leaf* (vector search → numbered `[n]` passages; needs a `retriever` + `collection_ids` in context). Grant it ONLY to agents that *are* the KB agent: the `kb_chat` presets and the `infer_modules` classifier (likewise `search_wiki`/`read_source` only to the wiki maintainer/reader agents — #537: `search_wiki` is a LEAF grep over wiki pages, and an agent holding it without `read_file` can't open the page it matched, follow a `[[wikilink]]`, or cite anything; the KB agent reaches the wiki through **`ask_wiki`**, which delegates the whole index-first navigation to the wiki reader in a throwaway context and returns a synthesized, cited answer — same delegate-for-context-economy shape as `ask_knowledge_base`, and non-recursive because the reader holds the leaves, not `ask_wiki`). Every *other* app agent gets **`ask_knowledge_base`** instead — it delegates the whole question to a `kb_chat` sub-agent (via the generic `run_subagent` bridge, wired for every app turn in `_send_into` + for workflows; KB-chat turns deliberately omit it so the KB agent can't recurse) and returns a synthesized, cited answer (non-streaming `answer_question`). The point of the sub-agent is **context isolation**: the noisy retrieval (multi-query/HyDE/rerank, raw passages, whole wiki pages) stays in the sub-agent's throwaway context, keeping the consumer's window lean — which matters most for small local models. It still relays the sub-agent's searches + reasoning live into the parent stream via `ctx.on_exec_output` (`answer_question(on_event=…)` + `kb_progress`) so the turn shows progress instead of stalling. **They can't be collapsed into one tool:** `ask_knowledge_base` spawns a KB agent, and that inner agent must search with a *non-delegating* tool (`kb_search`) or it would call `ask_knowledge_base` on itself forever — the leaf is irreducible. So when wiring a new app, list `ask_knowledge_base`, **never** `kb_search`/`search_wiki`/`ask_wiki` (those need a retriever/wiki context the app doesn't have and will fail without). **#537 — a KB turn's search allowances decide its TOOL SET, not just its refusals**: `kb_search`/`ask_wiki` capped at `0` are NOT granted for that turn (`agent/search_scope.py:tools_within_budget`, applied in the KB turn + `answer_question` + `AskKbSpec.allowed_tools`), and the allowance is stated in the prompt up front (`describe_budgets`) with off-sources still NAMED (#480). Granting-then-refusing was the actual defect: the refusal text says "answer now from what you have", which a model reads as *stop searching altogether*, so capping documents at 0 silenced the wiki too. There is no chunk/wiki/both ROUTING any more — `WikiAwareRunner` + the merge agent are retired; the agent picks a source per call. `lookup_glossary` is the exception — a cheap, deterministic, exact-key context-card lookup (no LLM, no retriever), so it's fine to grant any app directly.
 - **SourceDoc id is an opaque, slash-free token** (`kb/doc_id.encode_doc_id` = the natural key `{collection}/{user}/{path}` percent-encoded; specstar ids can't hold `/`). **Never parse it** — read `path`/`collection`/`user` from the record + `created_by` meta. `render_document` takes it as a query param (`GET /kb/documents?id=`); the FE treats it opaquely.
